WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.210
At first glance, Fortnite might just seem
like a typical video game, but what if I told

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:10.920
you that it’s actually just one small piece
of a $30 billion dollar empire that is set

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.990
to change gaming forever?

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.130
Fortnite is one of the most profitable games
ever made.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.009
But the company behind the game is just getting
started.

00:00:19.009 --> 00:00:23.320
Epic Games spent nearly 30 years laying the
groundwork that would allow them to create

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.470
the most fun, and most profitable games in
the world.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.410
Instead of simply making a great game, Epic
has built a network of businesses that virtually

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:37.520
guarantee continued growth for the company,
regardless of what game is most popular at

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.520
the moment.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.280
Tim Sweeney is the founder of Epic Games,
and he’s been methodically planning to take

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.120
over the gaming world for decades now, but
you probably don’t know much about him.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.880
That’s because he intentionally keeps a
very low profile and is extremely focused

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.470
on his vision for the future of gaming.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:00.190
Despite being a billionaire, he doesn’t
spend time partying on fancy yachts or hanging

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.190
out with celebrities.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.649
He’s on a mission to change the world of
gaming and fundamentally alter the way humans

00:01:05.649 --> 00:01:07.140
interact with each other.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.619
His ultimate goal is to create a fully immersive
metaverse, like the one seen in Ready Player

00:01:12.619 --> 00:01:14.749
One, and he’s already on the right track.

00:01:14.749 --> 00:01:19.420
He’s building all the fundamental infrastructure
that will be required to power something like

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.810
the Oasis and that’s starting to scare big
tech companies.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.049
Apple and Epic are battling it out in the
courts, but in order to understand what’s

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:31.479
really going on here, and why Tim is willing
to risk everything in order to take on big

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:36.780
tech, we really have to understand Tim as
a person and drill into what motivates him.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.979
Tim has developed some very unique skills
and discovered an incredible business model

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:42.000
in the process.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.090
His strategy has completely set Epic apart
from the competition and is truly one of the

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.859
most interesting technology stories of the
last 30 years.

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:54.729
Tim Sweeney was born in Potomac, Maryland
in 1970, and developed a love of computers

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:56.249
and engineering at a young age.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:02:01.420
He’d spend his free time disassembling lawn
mowers and playing Atari games, but computers

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.799
weren’t exactly widely available at that
time.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:08.479
Fortunately for Tim, he was lucky enough to
be born into a family that had access to cutting

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:09.479
edge technology.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.569
See, his dad worked at the Department of Defense
where his job was to create military maps

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:16.270
as part of the Intelligence Unit.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.680
This meant that his dad had access to the
best computers available at the time and could

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.409
see first hand the impact technology was going
to have on the world.

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:28.069
Additionally, Tim had a brother who was 10
years older than him who worked in the computer

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:32.650
industry and helped introduce Tim to programming
when he was just 11 years old.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.849
When Tim saw the flexibility of his brother’s
IBM computers, he was hooked.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:41.740
He was a natural programmer and started dedicating
all of his free time to learning about computers.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.090
Tim’s brother Steve clearly recognized the
raw programming talent Tim possessed, so he

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.590
purchased an Apple II.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.330
Tim immediately started building software
for the Apple II, and because his dad was

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.450
connected to the DOD, Tim was able to access
basic internet forums to ask questions about

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:57.900
problems he ran into.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.709
At that time, the internet was extremely small.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:05.930
You literally had to either work at a major
university or for the government to get access.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.660
So Tim was lucky to connect with these experts
directly.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.650
There are plenty of people with innate programming
skills, but that alone isn’t enough to become

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:14.959
a successful entrepreneur.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:18.959
You have to understand business and develop
an intuition for what your best opportunity

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:20.700
is at any given time.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.400
Tim started developing his business skills
while in high school.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.269
He was working a summer job at a hardware
store and realized that the only way for him

00:03:27.269 --> 00:03:30.450
to make more money was to work more hours
at the store.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.650
So he quit his job and set up a lawn mowing
business where he could make five times as

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.549
much money by setting his own prices and using
a tractor to get jobs done faster.

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:44.140
This idea of always seeking out the highest
leverage opportunity is at the core of most

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:49.159
entrepreneurship philosophy: create a system
that makes money so that your income no longer

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:51.120
requires your direct input.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.019
Tim realized this while still in high school,
so when he got to college he was in a prime

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:59.269
position to start working on the most scalable
business of all: software development.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:03.180
He went to the University of Maryland to study
engineering and wasn’t a particularly great

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.180
student.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.780
He spent most of his time working on computers
and the only courses he really enjoyed were

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.069
related to math and physics, which will play
a big role later in this story.

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:15.630
He needed to make money while in college,
but wanted to build his computer skills at

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.329
the same time, so he started a company called
Potomac Computer Systems.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.750
At that time, he hadn’t built a product
yet, he was just consulting for small businesses

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.350
to make sure they had usable computer systems.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:31.139
His new business made enough money to pay
for an IBM 286, which was a big step up in

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:32.690
terms of computing power.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:37.190
Even though Tim enjoyed programming on the
Apple II, more businesses used IBM computers

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.830
at the time, so that’s where the real money
could be made.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:44.240
At the time, writing software was extremely
cumbersome and nearly every business needed

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.210
custom software to run efficiently.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:51.830
This was before AWS, before GitHub, and even
before basic text editors existed like Vim

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:57.530
or eMacs, so Tim set out to create a text
editor that could speed up software development.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:02.380
But as was building the text editor, he realized
that he could build a simple video game using

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.780
the same tools, so he pivoted and launched
a game called ZZT.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.380
In past videos, I’ve talked about how tools
like Discord and Slack grew out of failed

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.640
attempts to make games, and this is kind of
the opposite scenario.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.330
Tim wanted to create a tool and wound up making
a game.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:21.880
And the game did well, at least given how
small the PC gaming market was at the time.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.729
This was 1991 after all.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:28.880
Interestingly, the “shareware” distribution
model that Tim used to sell ZZT was remarkably

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.139
similar to what Epic would use to sell Fortnite
over 25 years later.

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:37.449
He gave the basic version away for free, and
then had customers who liked the game pay

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:38.979
for extra features.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:43.720
Additionally, the user could edit the game
themselves, so anyone who installed ZZT could

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:48.090
create their own levels, much like Fortnite
Creator Mode, which we’ll get to in a minute.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:53.090
With ZZT selling well, Tim realized that he
should focus on game development and put aside

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.740
the consulting work he was doing before.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.229
So he changed the name of the company to Epic
MegaGames.

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:03.100
He was still distributing ZZT as “shareware”
which meant he had to personally mail physical

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.280
copies of the game to customers, but, he would
keep 100% of the revenue he generated.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.090
Most game developers focus on making their
games as good as possible, and then partnering

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:16.610
with a publisher like Sony, Microsoft, or
Nintendo to actually push their game out to

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:17.610
the public.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:22.009
Game publishing was still in its infancy though,
and the allure of keeping 100% of the profits

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:27.360
for Epic was too good to pass up, so Tim decided
to grow his team and publish more games directly

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.449
to customers.

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:32.550
Even though he was still in college, Tim hired
several key people to help him develop his

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:37.160
next game, which would be a 2D side-scrolling
game called Jill of the Jungle.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.440
This game looks extremely simple nowadays,
but at the time it was cutting edge.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.700
In order to make and sell this game, Tim hired
Cliff Bleszinski, who was just 17 years old

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:51.490
at the time, and Mark Rein, who had been working
at ID software making games like Doom.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:56.310
When people think of Doom today, they usually
think of the 2016 version for Xbox One and

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:02.810
PS4, but back in 1993, Doom was released for
MS-Dos computers and looks extremely simple

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:03.810
now.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.830
But Doom was revolutionary at the time, selling
millions of copies and introducing the world

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:11.870
to 3D video games that could be played on
a home computer for the first time.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:16.379
In many ways, Doom was just as big of a cultural
moment as Fortnite, completely dominating

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:20.940
the conversation about PC gaming for years
and inspiring tons of game developers to make

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.389
their own 3D shooters.

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:26.940
Tim wanted Epic to get in on the action and
make a 3D game like Doom, but that wouldn’t

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.120
be easy.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.159
Rendering 3D levels on basic home computing
hardware required some really complicated

00:07:32.159 --> 00:07:35.950
game programming that only ID software had
figured out at the time.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.180
But Tim saw this as an opportunity.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:42.759
He recognized that anyone who wanted to make
a modern video game would need to spend countless

00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:47.220
hours building the low-level rendering technology
to actually get the game to work.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.350
All this had to be done before they could
create levels or integrate a story or particular

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:56.130
game mechanics that gamers would actually
care about, so Tim set out to solve this problem

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.530
for the entire industry.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.550
Epic would focus on developing two pieces
of software.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:04.810
The first would be a game called Unreal that
would compete directly with Doom, but the

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:09.979
second would be a game engine that would allow
any game developer to make their own 3D game

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:12.569
without needing to do all that low-level programming.

00:08:12.569 --> 00:08:16.189
This product was called Unreal Engine, and
it’s still the core to Epic’s business

00:08:16.189 --> 00:08:17.189
model today.

00:08:17.189 --> 00:08:21.690
Unreal became a popular game soon after launch,
but all the marketing and promotion that Tim

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.740
did around the game really served to attract
developers to Unreal Engine.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.009
The video game industry was starting to really
take off in the late 90s and developers wanted

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:33.990
to focus on quickly releasing games without
building an entire game engine every time

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.300
they created a new title.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:41.029
Unreal Engine solved this problem perfectly
and went on to power games like Deus Ex, BioShock,

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:42.029
and Splinter Cell.

00:08:42.029 --> 00:08:46.029
This was a huge win for Tim Sweeney and the
entire Epic team, but getting developers to

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:48.350
make games with Unreal Engine was just the
beginning.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:52.680
They weren’t just speeding up development,
they were actually unlocking new markets for

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:53.860
game developers.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.620
See, by the mid-2000s, the gaming market had
fragmented.

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.500
Gaming consoles had taken off, but there wasn’t
a clear winner.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.200
The Playstation and Xbox both had significant
market share, and developers wanted everyone

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.170
to play their games, regardless of what hardware
they had.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:12.690
By building their games on top of Unreal Engine,
developers could release Playstation, Xbox,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.690
and PC versions of their games without much
extra work.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:20.320
Epic was making it easier for developers to
launch bigger games and make more money, so

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:24.440
these developers were happy to pay Epic a
small revenue share in exchange for use of

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:25.440
Unreal Engine.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.519
But Tim wanted to make sure that Unreal Engine
would always be the go-to platform for developing

00:09:29.519 --> 00:09:33.640
games and there was a new generation of gaming
hardware coming out.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:38.709
In 2005, Microsoft was going to release the
Xbox 360, and Tim knew that if Epic could

00:09:38.709 --> 00:09:43.100
create a flagship game that everyone played,
it would draw even more developers to the

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.100
Unreal Engine.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.760
So Epic made Gears of War.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:53.860
And it was huge.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.610
This game showcased everything that these
next generation consoles could do and highlighted

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.140
the power of Unreal Engine.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:03.120
Gears of War featured a bunch of new mechanics
that were only possible because so much of

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.570
the heavy lifting was done by Epic’s new
Unreal Engine 3.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.740
Major franchises like Borderlands and Mass
Effect used this new version to sell millions

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.940
of games and win dozens of awards.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:17.380
With Unreal Engine growing relentlessly, Tim
decided to take outside investment for the

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.130
first time in Epic’s history.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:24.660
In 2012, he sold 40% of his company to Tencent
for $330 million dollars.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.790
This new capital allowed Tim to refocus Epic
on what really mattered for long-term growth.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:33.579
Epic sold Gears of War to Microsoft and doubled
down on Unreal Engine.

00:10:33.579 --> 00:10:37.930
Tim’s vision for Unreal Engine expanded
to meet the needs of all modern developers.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:42.529
A game engine that only helped with rendering
and game interactions wasn’t enough anymore.

00:10:42.529 --> 00:10:47.680
Increasingly, games were played online, integrated
with payment processors, and needed distribution.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.240
So Epic would expand to offer all of that,
all for the low price of just a 5% royalty.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:58.440
But $330 million dollars is a lot of money,
and it’s natural for any company that gets

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:04.970
a huge cash influx like that to want to expand
even further, so Epic planned out a slew of

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:05.970
new games.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.170
First, they started working on a new Unreal
Tournament game, but they also wanted to compete

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:14.459
with League of Legends, so they built a game
called Paragon to get into the multiplayer

00:11:14.459 --> 00:11:16.470
online battleground space.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:21.310
Minecraft had also recently become very popular,
which motivated Epic to release a game that

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.980
merged building mechanics with tower defense.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:27.470
They called that game Fortnite, but at this
point the game didn’t look anything like

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.250
the Fortnite that’s popular now.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.829
But in 2017 everything changed for Epic.

00:11:31.829 --> 00:11:36.450
All three of those games got stuck in development
and couldn’t really find a strong audience.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:40.019
There were small beta tests, but nothing really
caught on.

00:11:40.019 --> 00:11:44.260
But then PlayerUnknown’s Battlegrounds came
out and it was a huge hit.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:48.740
The general idea of a battle royale had been
around for a while, but PUBG was the first

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.760
game to really bring the concept into the
mainstream.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.770
With Unreal Tournament, Paragon, and Fortnite
all struggling, Tim decided to pivot the company

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:58.160
to focus on battle royale.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.440
So the Unreal Tournament team took what the
Fortnite team had built so far and started

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.100
making an online battle royale mode for the
game.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:08.830
They sprinted hard to get Fortnite Battle
Royale out into the world, and they released

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:13.839
it in September of 2017, just 6 months after
PUBG was released.

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:18.620
The game was a massive success and got 10
million active players within just 2 weeks.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:23.290
There are a few key reasons why Fortnite was
so successful and they all built upon the

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:27.050
work Tim had done at Epic over the previous
25 years.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:31.290
First, Fortnite was immediately cross-platform,
which was only possible because of all the

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:36.450
resources Epic had poured into making Unreal
Engine platform independent from day one.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.420
Second, Fortnite was far less buggy than the
competition.

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:43.500
Since the Epic team knew the Unreal Engine
codebase inside and out, they were able to

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:48.300
make sure that the game could run reliably
without issues on all kinds of devices, even

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.710
older PCs.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:54.209
PUBG is actually built on Unreal Engine as
well, but their team didn’t have the resources

00:12:54.209 --> 00:12:57.820
or experience to utilize the engine to its
full potential.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:02.310
Lastly, Fortnite was free-to-play, which has
been Tim’s go to business model since he

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.290
released his first game ever.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.600
Epic had an entire team internally dedicated
to online services to help with things like

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.899
scaling servers and offering micro-transactions.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:16.610
Even though they didn’t charge for Fortnite
up-front, Epic still made more than $9 billion

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:18.290
dollars in its first two years.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:19.290
Incredible.

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:23.380
But Fortnite is just one game, and Tim has
seen gamers can move from one trend to another

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:24.699
really quickly before.

00:13:24.699 --> 00:13:29.470
The Gears of War sequels were never as profitable
as the first game, and betting all of Epic

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.490
on Fortnite staying popular was simply too
risky.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.860
So he focused on further diversification.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:38.480
Since millions of people had already installed
Fortnite, he made the decision to convert

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:42.430
the Fortnite launcher into a store where you
could buy all sorts of games.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:46.750
Valve’s Steam platform has been the dominant
PC game distributor for years, but they take

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:49.490
a 30% cut of revenue from game developers.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:55.390
Epic majorly undercut Valve, charging just
12%, which was a huge incentive to start selling

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:56.880
on the Epic Games Store.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.820
These royalty fees are a big point of contention
in many parts of Epic’s business now.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:05.579
Recently, they have been trying to get around
Apple’s 30% in-app purchase fee by allowing

00:14:05.579 --> 00:14:09.380
customers to pay with credit cards directly,
instead of going through Apple.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:13.779
This violates the App Store policies and has
led to a massive lawsuit between Apple and

00:14:13.779 --> 00:14:16.790
Epic Games, which is still going on as of
June 2021.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.630
It’s easy to cynically look at this battle
between two tech companies worth billions

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:24.240
of dollars and assume that they each just
want to make more money, and that’s not

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:25.639
entirely wrong.

00:14:25.639 --> 00:14:30.300
But Tim Sweeney does have a very specific
vision for Epic’s future that benefits from

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.090
an open internet with low interchange fees.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.820
See, Tim wants to create a metaverse.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:39.730
He wants Fortnite players to be able to take
the content they’ve purchased into Minecraft

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.690
and vice versa, but that’s just the start.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:46.600
Increasingly, Fortnite isn’t just about
competition, it’s become a place to hang

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:50.740
out and socialize, build things with your
friends, and see concerts.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:55.040
As these games become more immersive, they
start to behave more like social networks

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.110
than traditional single-player games.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:01.290
Tim thinks that the future of human interaction
will take place in a virtual space powered

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:02.860
by Unreal Engine.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:07.550
Simply put, being beholden to Apple or Google
will make it significantly harder to scale

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.000
as quickly as he would like.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:13.550
Tim has been working on Epic for 30 years
now and he’s in some ways just beginning

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:14.720
his journey.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:19.220
The company has expanded significantly with
four key business lines built around Unreal

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:20.220
Engine.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:23.779
Fortnite brings in money directly and serves
as the perfect showcase for what’s possible

00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:24.949
with Unreal Engine.

00:15:24.949 --> 00:15:29.100
Epic Online Services helps new multiplayer
games scale when they start growing and need

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.139
more servers.

00:15:30.139 --> 00:15:34.680
The Epic Games Store now lets Epic distribute
not only their new games, but also games from

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:35.910
other publishers.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:41.120
And Epic also has their own studio and publisher
for building and releasing new games.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:46.120
This flywheel is incredibly powerful, because
all of the business lines feed into one another.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:50.720
Epic can use profits from Fortnite to help
grow their Game Store and then use profits

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:53.250
from the Game Store to develop new games.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.850
The experience they get from building Fortnite
informs how they think about the next version

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.360
of Unreal Engine and so on.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.710
It’s a virtuous cycle that helps keep the
company growing indefinitely.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:02.560
And that’s awesome.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:06.709
